      "math_academy_id": "q-161046",
      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨\\text{__________},\\,\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(z,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=\\sqrt{z-1}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [1,10].$ What is the first component of $r?$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14177361/assets/04de4ea7dc617be1429c596812ce4f450c2cdaafd8d94d86f75e68a6743acba9.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(z)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(z)=\\sqrt{z-1}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [1,10].$ \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [1,10],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $r$ is $\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
